{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310715,"nonce":"GW2wS2fAyFJEZmeSWiPoz0fnHII=","title":"`arr.length` and similar stdlib MIR-wrapper-backed property/method calls registered the wrapper name (e.g. `array_length`) as a Cranelift ...","message":"FIXED in `HirToMirContext::get_or_register_extern_function`. Names matching known stdlib MIR-wrapper patterns (`array_*`, `String_*`, `Tensor_*`, `QTensor_*`) or flagged via the stdlib runtime mapping's `is_mir_wrapper` field are now routed through `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` instead of being declared as externs. The forward-ref path produces a function that shares its Cranelift FuncId with the wrapper's body when the stdlib MIR module is merged.\n\nWhy this matters: Cranelift's `Module::define_function` refuses to define a body on a FuncId previously declared as `Linkage::Import`. The error reads: `Invalid to define identifier declared as an import: array_length`. The backend swallows it as `[W0020] cannot compile ...` then panics at runtime with `can't resolve symbol`.\n\nHow to apply: when adding a new stdlib MIR wrapper (a function built via `builder.begin_function(\"name\")` in `compiler/src/stdlib/*.rs`), make sure its runtime mapping entry in `runtime_mapping.rs` either uses the `mir_wrapper` macro variant OR its name fits one of the recognised prefixes the central detector covers. If you can't change the mapping, the prefix check is your fallback.\n\nWhy the prefix list is in `get_or_register_extern_function` (and not in every call site): there are at least three lowering paths that resolve property/method calls through the stdlib runtime name (`try_property_call_via_stdlib`, `lower_field_access_for_class`'s stdlib-property branch, and various extern-method redirects). Centralising the check at the registration site means new paths inherit the routing automatically.\n\nSurfaced by: llama-chat (Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M) after [[bugs-iface-arg-to-instance-method]] was fixed, when `LlamaArch.build` could finally run and reference `blocks.length`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_stdlib_mir_wrapper_import_clash` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}